11:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L21",null,{"guide":{"date":"May 30–June 7, 1862","place":"The Shenandoah Valley and Page Valley in Virginia, from Winchester and Strasburg south along the Valley Turnpike and Luray Road to Harrisonburg, Cross Keys, and Port Republic","result":"Jackson escaped the attempted United States convergence at Strasburg and withdrew south under pressure from Frémont west of Massanutten Mountain and Shields to the east. Confederate bridge destruction, the paved Valley Turnpike, heavy rain, and hesitant Federal movement helped keep the two pursuing columns apart. Jackson then turned southeast at Harrisonburg and concentrated near Port Republic, where he occupied a central position from which he could fight the separated columns in turn. The June 6 rear-guard engagement near Harrisonburg delayed Frémont but cost the Confederacy Turner Ashby, who was killed while leading infantry on foot. The battles of Cross Keys and Port Republic fall outside this episode and begin the next installment.","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The retreat from the lower Valley and approach to Cross Keys and Port Republic","note":"The narrative resumes on Friday evening, May 30, and ends with the armies positioned near Cross Keys and Port Republic on June 7. It does not narrate the June 8 Battle of Cross Keys or June 9 Battle of Port Republic."},{"label":"Operational result","value":"Jackson escaped the Strasburg trap and kept Frémont and Shields separated","note":"The United States columns eventually reached the Strasburg area after Jackson had cleared it. Their convergence therefore closed behind, rather than around, the Confederate army."},{"label":"Strategic geography","value":"Massanutten Mountain separated the two Federal pursuits","note":"Frémont followed the Valley Turnpike west of the mountain. Shields moved south through the Page, or Luray, Valley on the east. Crossings between the valleys were limited, so roads, bridges, and timing controlled whether the columns could unite."},{"label":"Road advantage","value":"Jackson used the macadamized Valley Turnpike while Shields faced dirt roads","note":"Persistent rain made Shields’s route especially difficult. The road difference did not alone determine the campaign, but it materially favored Jackson’s retreat and complicated coordination between the Federal columns."},{"label":"May 31 failure","value":"Neither Federal arm reached Strasburg in time","note":"The transcript says Shields stopped because he accepted an unfounded report that James Longstreet had arrived nearby from Richmond. Frémont’s vanguard ended May 31 about five miles short of Strasburg despite the expectation that it would reach the town that day."},{"label":"Longstreet report","value":"False or unsubstantiated intelligence in the episode’s account","note":"Longstreet had not reinforced Jackson near Front Royal or Strasburg. The guide treats Shields’s belief as a reported explanation for delay, not as an actual Confederate movement."},{"label":"Confederate impediments","value":"A seven-mile wagon train and nearly 2,000 prisoners slowed the withdrawal","note":"These are the episode’s figures. The 21st Virginia guarded the prisoners. The exact length of a moving train and number present at any instant cannot be reconstructed with precision."},{"label":"Forced march","value":"Fifty minutes marching and no more than ten minutes rest in each hour","note":"The episode describes Jackson enforcing this pace on May 31. It also says the Stonewall Brigade and 1st Maryland marched about 35 miles without food, while the 2nd Virginia covered about 42 miles and had gone two days without rations."},{"label":"June 1 at Strasburg","value":"Jackson held the road open until his detached troops arrived","note":"Frémont’s advance skirmished with the Confederate blocking force west of town beginning about 7:00 a.m. The Stonewall Brigade and 1st Maryland entered Strasburg around noon and passed safely through the junction."},{"label":"Federal contact date","value":"Sources describe the failed convergence with slightly different date language","note":"The episode emphasizes the June 1 skirmishing and Confederate passage through Strasburg. The U.S. Army campaign brochure says the Federal vanguards met near Strasburg on June 2. These statements can coexist: the arms approached on June 1 and completed contact only after Jackson had escaped."},{"label":"Shields’s second plan","value":"Move south through Luray to reach Jackson’s front while Frémont followed his rear","note":"McDowell consented without enthusiasm. The plan was operationally plausible, but rain, poor roads, destroyed bridges, distance, and divided command prevented timely cooperation."},{"label":"Condition of Jackson’s army","value":"Severe straggling and loss of unit cohesion","note":"The episode uses Federal and Confederate accounts describing men, equipment, clothing, and broken vehicles along the road. It quotes a 27th Virginia officer estimating at least 1,000 men of the Stonewall Brigade had fallen out by June 3; that is a contemporary estimate, not a verified casualty return."},{"label":"Episode title quotation","value":"Jackson’s June 2 letter to his wife","note":"The transcript quotes Jackson writing that the enemy tried to reach his rear on both flanks but that God had guided him and saved him from their grasp. The guide paraphrases the letter in the timeline rather than extending the quotation."},{"label":"Richmond command change","value":"Robert E. Lee replaced the wounded Joseph E. Johnston","note":"Johnston was seriously wounded at Seven Pines on May 31. Lee assumed command of the force defending Richmond on June 1. Jackson did not yet know of the change when he wrote Johnston during the retreat."},{"label":"Bridge campaign","value":"Confederate cavalry destroyed crossings to obstruct a Federal junction","note":"The transcript says Jackson sent cavalry to burn two bridges and that the bridge at Conrad’s Store was destroyed the next day. It does not name the first two crossings, so the guide does not invent names. On the Valley Turnpike, Ashby’s rear guard also contested bridges as Frémont’s troops tried to save them."},{"label":"Turner Ashby’s promotion","value":"He received notice of promotion to brigadier general on June 3","note":"Contemporary and later sources differ in how they style his rank because the Confederate Senate did not confirm the appointment before his death. The guide uses “Brig. Gen.” in the command display but records the qualification here."},{"label":"Rain and exhaustion","value":"Men marched hungry, barefoot, sleep-deprived, and repeatedly soaked","note":"The episode’s claim that the first four days of June produced more than half a normal June’s rainfall is attributed to an unnamed weather station and has not been independently verified here. The broader fact of sustained heavy rain is supported by the campaign record and eyewitness accounts."},{"label":"Port Republic decision","value":"Jackson chose a central position between the separated Federal columns","note":"Control of the river crossings near Port Republic allowed him to prevent Frémont from readily joining Shields. Brown’s Gap also provided an eastern route over the Blue Ridge toward the Virginia Central Railroad and Richmond if withdrawal became necessary."},{"label":"June 5 turn","value":"The Valley Army left the turnpike at Harrisonburg for Port Republic","note":"This southeast movement placed Ewell on the approach from Harrisonburg near Cross Keys and the remainder of Jackson’s force closer to the Port Republic crossings."},{"label":"Engagement near Harrisonburg","value":"A sharp Confederate rear-guard success on June 6","note":"Federal cavalry from the 1st New Jersey attacked toward Good’s Farm and was followed by fighting involving the 13th Pennsylvania Reserves. Confederate infantry, including the 58th Virginia and 1st Maryland, checked the advance. Exact casualty totals vary by account and are not stated in the episode."},{"label":"Ashby’s death","value":"Killed near Good’s Farm while leading infantry on foot","note":"His horse was shot, after which he rose and urged the men forward before a bullet struck him in the chest or side. The identity of the person who fired the fatal shot is unknown. Claims for the Pennsylvania Bucktails and suggestions of friendly fire remain unproven."},{"label":"June 7 reconnaissance","value":"Milroy discovered that Jackson intended to fight","note":"Frémont ordered Brig. Gen. Robert H. Milroy toward Port Republic. Near Cross Keys he encountered Ewell’s troops, obeyed orders not to start a general engagement, and withdrew with evidence that the Confederate retreat had ended."},{"label":"Exposed eastern approach","value":"Jackson left only a light screen at the South River fords","note":"The episode identifies one company of the 2nd Virginia Infantry at the fords and three companies of the 7th Virginia Cavalry farther out. This thin security of the Shields approach created the danger that opens the next episode."},{"label":"Important leader losses","value":"Turner Ashby was killed; Joseph E. Johnston was seriously wounded elsewhere","note":"Ashby is the only senior commander killed within the episode’s geographic and chronological action. Johnston was wounded at Seven Pines outside Richmond and survived; his wound matters here because it produced the command change to Lee."},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Strasburg, Frémont, Luray, Ewell, Henry Kyd Douglas, and James K. Edmondson","note":"The automated transcript renders these in forms including “Strasbourg,” “Fremont,” “LeRae,” “Yule,” “Henry Kye Douglas,” and “James K. Edmonton.” The guide uses standard historical spellings. “Lante Blackford” is rendered as Lanty Blackford."},{"label":"Book recommendation","value":"“Lee’s Lieutenants: A Study in Command,” by Douglas Southall Freeman","note":"The hosts specifically recommend the one-volume abridgment edited by Stephen W. Sears while noting that the original work is three volumes."}],"maps":[{"title":"May 30–June 7: the failed trap and retreat to Port Republic","caption":"Jackson withdrew south along the Valley Turnpike, passed through Strasburg before the Federal columns could close, and then turned southeast from Harrisonburg toward Port Republic. Frémont pursued west of Massanutten Mountain while Shields moved through the Luray Valley to the east. This operational map is schematic: routes, dates, distances, waterways, and force locations are simplified and approximate.","source":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-5.pdf","image":"/images/ep152-strasburg-port-republic-operation.svg","alt":"Schematic operational map showing Jackson retreating from Winchester through Strasburg and Harrisonburg to Port Republic while Frémont and Shields pursue on opposite sides of Massanutten Mountain."},{"title":"June 6: engagement near Harrisonburg","caption":"Frémont’s advance pressed southeast from Harrisonburg. The 1st New Jersey Cavalry attacked the Confederate rear guard, and the fighting expanded when the 13th Pennsylvania Reserves came forward. The 58th Virginia and 1st Maryland helped check the Federal advance near Good’s Farm and Chestnut Ridge. Ashby was killed after his horse fell and he led on foot. This is a schematic phase map, not a surveyed plot; the exact fatal-shot location and shooter are unknown.","source":"https://www.dhr.virginia.gov/pdf_files/SpecialCollections/RM-052_Historic_AH_Survey_Rockingham_2000_TRACERIES_report.pdf","image":"/images/ep152-harrisonburg-skirmish.svg","alt":"Schematic map of the June 6 engagement southeast of Harrisonburg showing the Federal advance, Confederate infantry counterattack, and approximate area of Turner Ashby’s death."},{"title":"Hotchkiss campaign maps of the Shenandoah Valley","caption":"This 1880 proof sheet, based in part on Confederate engineer surveys and Jedediah Hotchkiss’s campaign work, contains nine maps. The upper Valley panel shows Harrisonburg, Cross Keys, Swift Run Gap, and Port Republic, while the overview places the movements in the broader Valley. It is a later historical compilation, not a map drawn during the May 30–June 7 retreat.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448346/","image":"/images/ep152-hotchkiss-valley-campaign-map.jpg","alt":"Proof sheet of nine historical maps illustrating Stonewall Jackson’s 1862 Shenandoah Valley Campaign."}],"moments":[{"time":"0:58","audioTime":58,"title":"The race for Strasburg resumes","detail":"The episode returns to Friday evening, May 30, with Jackson’s Valley Army trying to reach Strasburg before Frémont from the west and Shields from the east could close the intended trap."},{"time":"1:30","audioTime":90,"title":"Both Federal columns appear closer","detail":"On Friday evening the two Federal forces seemed better placed to reach Strasburg than Jackson, making encirclement appear possible."},{"time":"1:42","audioTime":102,"title":"Frémont marches twenty miles","detail":"The episode credits Frémont’s men with covering about twenty miles in twelve hours on May 30 despite poor condition and heavy rain."},{"time":"2:02","audioTime":122,"title":"Shields promises activity","detail":"Shields told McDowell that rapid action and direct pressure on Frémont could capture or destroy Jackson’s force."},{"time":"2:18","audioTime":138,"title":"Shields stops on May 31","detail":"The episode attributes his inactivity to a false report that Longstreet had arrived nearby from Richmond, although only Ashby’s small cavalry force blocked the immediate road to Strasburg."},{"time":"2:54","audioTime":174,"title":"Frémont also falls short","detail":"Darkness halted Frémont’s vanguard about five miles west of Strasburg after it failed to meet the expected May 31 arrival time."},{"time":"3:12","audioTime":192,"title":"The Confederate column starts south","detail":"Jackson’s men left the vicinity of Stephenson’s Depot before dawn, but a long captured wagon train and nearly 2,000 prisoners guarded by the 21st Virginia slowed the march."},{"time":"3:57","audioTime":237,"title":"Jackson imposes a relentless pace","detail":"The column marched fifty minutes of each hour and rested no more than ten, while Jackson rode with or ahead of the vanguard."},{"time":"4:19","audioTime":259,"title":"Detached brigades lag near Newtown","detail":"The Stonewall Brigade and 1st Maryland, recalled from the Harpers Ferry and Martinsburg demonstrations, stopped near Newtown at 10:00 p.m. with more men strung out behind than remaining in ranks."},{"time":"5:39","audioTime":339,"title":"Hunger compounds the forced march","detail":"The episode says the exhausted men had marched roughly 35 miles without food, while the 2nd Virginia had gone about 42 miles and two days without rations."},{"time":"5:53","audioTime":353,"title":"Jackson holds at Strasburg","detail":"On June 1 Jackson kept the road open until the Stonewall Brigade and 1st Maryland could rejoin the main army."},{"time":"6:06","audioTime":366,"title":"Frémont’s advance skirmishes","detail":"Fighting west of Strasburg began about 7:00 a.m. and grew to include artillery, but Frémont did not force a full-scale action against the Confederate blocking force."},{"time":"7:24","audioTime":444,"title":"The last detached troops enter Strasburg","detail":"At noon the Stonewall Brigade and 1st Maryland marched into town and passed through the narrowing escape corridor before the Federal columns could unite."},{"time":"9:05","audioTime":545,"title":"Shields proposes a second interception","detail":"After the Strasburg opportunity passed, Shields obtained McDowell’s consent to move south by the Luray Road and try to reach Jackson’s front while Frémont pursued from behind."},{"time":"10:14","audioTime":614,"title":"The Valley Army nears physical collapse","detail":"By June 2 sustained marching, hunger, lack of sleep, rain, and straggling had broken down unit cohesion. Federal pursuers found discarded clothing, weapons, equipment, and disabled vehicles along the route."},{"time":"11:21","audioTime":681,"title":"Jackson rebukes a brigade commander","detail":"Henry Kyd Douglas recalled Jackson demanding that a frustrated colonel restore his brigade or yield command to someone who could."},{"time":"11:50","audioTime":710,"title":"Jackson writes the episode’s title line","detail":"In a letter to his wife, Jackson described retiring before enemies moving on both flanks and attributed his escape to divine guidance."},{"time":"12:09","audioTime":729,"title":"Jackson writes an outdated commander","detail":"He reported his withdrawal to Joseph E. Johnston without knowing Johnston had been seriously wounded at Seven Pines and Robert E. Lee had assumed command near Richmond."},{"time":"12:25","audioTime":745,"title":"Rain makes bridges decisive","detail":"Jackson used cavalry detachments to destroy crossings that might let Shields unite with Frémont, including the bridge at Conrad’s Store after two earlier, unnamed spans."},{"time":"13:12","audioTime":792,"title":"Ashby contests the turnpike bridges","detail":"On June 3 the cavalry rear guard tried to destroy successive bridges just before Frémont’s advance could seize them. Ashby received notice of his appointment as brigadier general that morning."},{"time":"14:00","audioTime":840,"title":"Straggling worsens by New Market","detail":"Letters from James K. Edmondson, James Dinwiddie, Lanty Blackford, and Randolph Fairfax describe broken ranks, bleeding feet, hunger, standing water, and only four or five hours of sleep per day."},{"time":"16:04","audioTime":964,"title":"Port Republic becomes the key position","detail":"Jackson recognized that control of the crossings near Port Republic could keep the Federal columns separated and allow him to fight one before the other arrived."},{"time":"17:23","audioTime":1043,"title":"Jackson turns east at Harrisonburg","detail":"On June 5 the Valley Army left the Valley Turnpike for the road to Port Republic. Frémont reached Harrisonburg the following day."},{"time":"17:37","audioTime":1057,"title":"Turner Ashby is killed","detail":"During the June 6 rear-guard fighting southeast of Harrisonburg, Ashby’s horse was shot. He rose, urged nearby infantry forward, and was killed by a bullet through the chest or side."},{"time":"18:47","audioTime":1128,"title":"Milroy probes toward Cross Keys","detail":"On June 7 Frémont sent Robert H. Milroy toward Port Republic. Milroy encountered Ewell’s troops near Cross Keys, avoided a general battle as ordered, and reported that Jackson had stopped retreating."},{"time":"19:31","audioTime":1171,"title":"Jackson divides his defensive responsibilities","detail":"Ewell covered the western approach near Cross Keys while Jackson placed the rest of the army above Port Republic, but only a small infantry and cavalry screen watched the South River fords against Shields."},{"time":"20:17","audioTime":1217,"title":"The armies rest within striking distance","detail":"The Confederates gained nearly two days of rest around Port Republic while Frémont and Shields moved closer, setting the stage for the next episode’s battles."},{"time":"20:37","audioTime":1237,"title":"The episode recommends “Lee’s Lieutenants”","detail":"The hosts recommend Douglas Southall Freeman’s command study, particularly the one-volume abridgment edited by Stephen W. Sears."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"President Abraham Lincoln / Secretary Edwin M. Stanton","command":"National strategic direction","subordinates":"Set the attempted convergence against Jackson and communicated separately with McDowell and Frémont, contributing to a divided field command"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Irvin McDowell","command":"Department of the Rappahannock","subordinates":"Supervised Shields from the east and consented to the renewed movement south through the Luray Valley after Jackson escaped Strasburg"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. James Shields","command":"Division under McDowell, eastern arm","subordinates":"Advanced from Front Royal through the Luray Valley; delayed on May 31 and later attempted to get ahead of Jackson near Port Republic"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. John C. Frémont","command":"Mountain Department, western arm","subordinates":"Approached Strasburg from the west, pursued south along the Valley Turnpike, reached Harrisonburg on June 6, and sent Milroy toward Cross Keys on June 7"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George D. Bayard","command":"Cavalry brigade from McDowell, attached to Frémont","subordinates":"Temporarily joined Frémont after Strasburg and directed mounted troops pressing Jackson’s rear, including the 1st New Jersey Cavalry in the June 6 action near Harrisonburg"},{"commander":"Col. Sir Percy Wyndham","command":"1st New Jersey Cavalry","subordinates":"Led the cavalry attack near Good’s Farm and was captured when the charge struck the Confederate rear guard"},{"commander":"Lt. Col. Thomas L. Kane","command":"13th Pennsylvania Reserves, “Bucktails” detachment","subordinates":"Entered the June 6 fighting after the cavalry action and engaged Confederate infantry near Chestnut Ridge"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Robert H. Milroy","command":"Brigade, Frémont’s army","subordinates":"Reconnoitered toward Cross Keys on June 7, met Ewell’s troops, and withdrew rather than bring on a general engagement"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Army of the Valley","subordinates":"Directed the retreat through Strasburg, ordered bridge destruction, turned southeast at Harrisonburg, and concentrated around Port Republic"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Richard S. Ewell","command":"Division commander","subordinates":"Helped cover the June 1 passage at Strasburg and by June 7 occupied the approach near Cross Keys between Frémont and Port Republic"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Charles S. Winder","command":"Jackson’s division / Stonewall Brigade","subordinates":"Brought the exhausted brigade south from the Harpers Ferry demonstration and through Strasburg at noon on June 1"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Turner Ashby","command":"Cavalry and rear guard","subordinates":"Screened the retreat, contested bridge crossings, and led the June 6 rear-guard action near Harrisonburg, where he was killed"},{"commander":"Col. Bradley T. Johnson","command":"1st Maryland Infantry, Confederate States","subordinates":"Rejoined Jackson through Strasburg and fought with the rear guard near Harrisonburg on June 6"},{"commander":"Col. Samuel H. Letcher","command":"58th Virginia Infantry","subordinates":"Commanded the regiment engaged with Federal cavalry and the Pennsylvania Bucktails near Good’s Farm and Chestnut Ridge"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","role":"Confederate commander who escaped the Strasburg convergence, withdrew up the Valley, and chose the central position at Port Republic","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep152-stonewall-jackson.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018670326/"},{"name":"Turner Ashby","role":"Confederate cavalry and rear-guard commander killed during the June 6 engagement southeast of Harrisonburg","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep152-turner-ashby.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2002725241/"},{"name":"Richard S. Ewell","role":"Division commander who helped cover Jackson’s retreat and blocked the western approach to Port Republic near Cross Keys","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Charles S. Winder","role":"Stonewall Brigade commander whose exhausted men made the final approach to Strasburg on June 1","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Bradley T. Johnson","role":"Commander of the Confederate 1st Maryland Infantry during the forced march and Harrisonburg rear-guard action","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Samuel H. Letcher","role":"Colonel commanding the 58th Virginia Infantry in the June 6 fighting near Harrisonburg","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Henry Kyd Douglas","role":"Jackson staff officer whose recollection describes broken unit cohesion and Jackson’s anger during the retreat","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John C. Frémont","role":"Mountain Department commander who approached Strasburg from the west and pursued Jackson south along the Valley Turnpike","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep152-john-fremont.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2017896011/"},{"name":"James Shields","role":"Division commander who approached from Front Royal and then moved south through the Luray Valley to get ahead of Jackson","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep152-james-shields-winchester.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2001700087/"},{"name":"Irvin McDowell","role":"Department commander supervising Shields and coordinating the eastern arm of the attempted convergence","side":"United States"},{"name":"George D. Bayard","role":"McDowell cavalry brigade commander temporarily operating with Frémont whose mounted advance pressed Jackson’s rear near Harrisonburg","side":"United States"},{"name":"Sir Percy Wyndham","role":"Colonel of the 1st New Jersey Cavalry captured during the June 6 attack near Good’s Farm","side":"United States"},{"name":"Thomas L. Kane","role":"Lieutenant colonel who led the Pennsylvania Bucktails detachment in the infantry phase of the Harrisonburg engagement","side":"United States"},{"name":"Robert H. Milroy","role":"Brigade commander whose June 7 reconnaissance found Ewell near Cross Keys and established that Jackson intended to fight","side":"United States"},{"name":"Joseph E. Johnston","role":"Confederate commander seriously wounded at Seven Pines; Jackson wrote to him before learning that command near Richmond had changed","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Confederate general who assumed command of the force defending Richmond on June 1 after Johnston was wounded","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"Brig. Gen. Turner Ashby","side":"Confederacy","role":"Cavalry commander and rear-guard leader","outcome":"Killed in action on June 6, 1862, near Good’s Farm southeast of Harrisonburg after his horse was shot and he led infantry forward on foot. The fatal shooter is unknown."},{"name":"Gen. Joseph E. Johnston","side":"Confederacy","role":"Commander of the Confederate force defending Richmond","outcome":"Seriously wounded at Seven Pines on May 31, outside this episode’s Valley operations; he survived. His incapacity led to Robert E. Lee assuming command on June 1."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep152-hotchkiss-valley-campaign-map.jpg","alt":"Nine-map proof sheet illustrating Jackson’s 1862 Valley Campaign.","caption":"Jedediah Hotchkiss and D. C. Humphreys prepared this 1880 proof sheet for William Allan’s campaign history. It includes an overview of the Valley and a panel of the Harrisonburg–Cross Keys–Port Republic region.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448346/"},{"image":"/images/ep152-turner-ashby.jpg","alt":"Engraved portrait of Confederate cavalry commander Turner Ashby.","caption":"Ashby directed the Confederate rear guard during the retreat. He was killed near Harrisonburg on June 6 after a cavalry clash expanded into an infantry engagement.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2002725241/"},{"image":"/images/ep152-stonewall-jackson.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era photographic portrait of Confederate general Thomas J. Stonewall Jackson.","caption":"Jackson used the Valley Turnpike, bridge destruction, and the geography around Massanutten to escape the attempted convergence and reach Port Republic.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018670326/"},{"image":"/images/ep152-john-fremont.jpg","alt":"Photographic portrait of United States general John C. Frémont.","caption":"Frémont approached Strasburg from the west and then pursued Jackson south on the Valley side of Massanutten Mountain.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2017896011/"},{"image":"/images/ep152-james-shields-winchester.jpg","alt":"Nineteenth-century lithograph depicting United States general James Shields at Winchester.","caption":"This historical print depicts Shields at the March 1862 Battle of Winchester, not during the May–June pursuit. In this episode he commanded the eastern Federal column moving through the Luray Valley.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2001700087/"},{"image":"/images/ep152-strasburg-port-republic-operation.svg","alt":"Schematic map of the attempted Federal convergence and Jackson’s retreat to Port Republic.","caption":"The operational schematic shows why Strasburg, the two roads south, Massanutten Mountain, and the Port Republic crossings governed the pursuit.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/jackson1862-valley-campaign.htm"},{"image":"/images/ep152-harrisonburg-skirmish.svg","alt":"Schematic phase map of the June 6 engagement near Harrisonburg.","caption":"The phase schematic shows the Federal advance from Harrisonburg, the Confederate rear-guard position, and the approximate area where Ashby was killed.","source":"https://www.dhr.virginia.gov/historic-registers/115-5063/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 152 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2016/05/08/civil-war-podcast-episode-152/"},{"label":"National Park Service: Jackson’s 1862 Campaign in the Shenandoah Valley","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/jackson1862-valley-campaign.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Cross Keys","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/battle-of-cross-keys.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Port Republic","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/battle-of-port-republic.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Virginia Campaigns, March–August 1862","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-5.pdf"},{"label":"Primary source collection: Official Records, Series I, Volume XII, Part I","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/015"},{"label":"Primary correspondence collection: Official Records, Series I, Volume XII, Part III","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/018"},{"label":"Encyclopedia Virginia: Shenandoah Valley Campaign of 1862","url":"https://encyclopediavirginia.org/entries/shenandoah-valley-campaign-of-1862/"},{"label":"Encyclopedia Virginia: Turner Ashby","url":"https://encyclopediavirginia.org/entries/ashby-turner-1828-1862/"},{"label":"Shenandoah Valley Battlefields National Historic District: Turner Ashby","url":"https://www.shenandoahatwar.org/turner-ashby"},{"label":"Virginia Department of Historic Resources: Turner Ashby Monument","url":"https://www.dhr.virginia.gov/historic-registers/115-5063/"},{"label":"Virginia DHR: Historic Architectural Survey of Rockingham County","url":"https://www.dhr.virginia.gov/pdf_files/SpecialCollections/RM-052_Historic_AH_Survey_Rockingham_2000_TRACERIES_report.pdf"},{"label":"University of Virginia Valley of the Shadow: June 1862 soldier diary","url":"https://valley.lib.virginia.edu/mod/AD1008"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Hotchkiss campaign map proof sheet","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448346/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Turner Ashby engraving","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2002725241/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018670326/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: John C. Frémont portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2017896011/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: James Shields at Winchester print","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2001700087/"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
